================================================================================
5. QSPI firmware flashing from Linux (over Ethernet, no USB/recovery)
================================================================================
   STATUS: ✅ DONE — implemented AND hardware-verified end-to-end (2026-06).
   Was NOT possible on prior image — capsule support was compiled OUT of UEFI.

   HARDWARE VERIFICATION (2026-06, lumen-orin-nano-8gb):
     - Rebuilt UEFI with capsule support; QSPI-flashed lumen firmware via recovery
       (flash-jetson skill, --qspi-only). OsIndicationsSupported went 0x41 -> 0x45
       (gained bit 0x04 FILE_CAPSULE_DELIVERY).
     - Ran apply_qspi_capsule.sh: staged tegra-bl.cap on the active-slot ESP, armed
       OsIndications 0x04, rebooted. UEFI wrote the inactive QSPI A/B slot, flipped
       the active slot (A -> B), and booted from it. ESRT reported
       last_attempt_status=0 (SUCCESS), last_attempt_version=0x240550 (our monotonic
       capsule version). Re-running is idempotent (no-op success, no flip).
     - The runtime path (/dev/efi_capsule_loader) returns EINVAL on Orin —
       capsule-on-disk is the ONLY working Linux path, as implemented.

   DONE IN REPO:
     ✅ a. edk2-boot-strip.cfg: CONFIG_FIRMWARE_CAPSULE_SUPPORTED=y
     ✅ c. tegra-uefi-capsules bbappend: CAPSULE_FW_VERSION now MONOTONIC per build
           (0x240500 + git commit count); --lsv kept at stock 0x240500. Override
           via CAPSULE_FW_VERSION=... to force re-apply of an unchanged tree.
     ✅ e. lumen-image-minimal.bb: added kernel-module-fat + kernel-module-vfat so
           the running OS can mount the ESP (NLS cp437/iso8859-1 already builtin=y).
     ✅ apply_qspi_capsule.sh rewritten to the WORKING capsule-on-disk method
           (ensure vfat -> mount active ESP -> EFI/UpdateCapsule -> arm OsIndications
           -> reboot -> verify ESRT). Verifies staging by sha/efivar READBACK (not
           heredoc exit code), and auto-pushes fat.ko/vfat.ko to a not-yet-updated
           rootfs that still lacks the modules.

   GOTCHAS / NOTES for the field:
     - First capsule-capable UEFI install is itself a QSPI write the OLD (capsule-less)
       UEFI cannot do from Linux — do ONE recovery-mode flash (flash-jetson --qspi-only)
       to seed it; afterwards all QSPI updates work from Linux via apply_qspi_capsule.sh.
     - TnSpec/product lock: TegraPlatformSpec is a NV UEFI var created LOCK_ON_CREATE
       by the first firmware to boot. If a board was previously flashed with a DIFFERENT
       product's firmware (e.g. meta-helios), that var stays locked to the old product
       and a bootloader-only QSPI flash does NOT erase the varstore. The capsule FMP
       check then fails with last_attempt_status=0x1807 (IMAGE_NOT_IN_PACKAGE) because
       the package TnSpec doesn't match. The matcher falls back to the NO_LOCK
       TegraPlatformCompatSpec, so deleting that var (it regenerates from current boot
       config on next boot) restores a match. A factory lumen-first board is unaffected.
     - fw_version (ESRT) stays 0x240500: it is read from the VER partition = the L4T
       BSP version (36.5.0), identical across lumen rebuilds. The monotonic 0x240550 is
       the CAPSULE header version (last_attempt_version / the UEFI version gate), which
       is the value that actually advances per build.

   ROOT CAUSE (now fixed):
     meta-lumen/recipes-bsp/uefi/edk2-firmware-tegra/edk2-boot-strip.cfg
       "# CONFIG_FIRMWARE_CAPSULE_SUPPORTED is not set"   <-- capsule stripped out

   TO ENABLE QSPI FLASHING FROM LINUX — do all of:

   a. Re-enable capsule support in UEFI:
      - edk2-boot-strip.cfg: set CONFIG_FIRMWARE_CAPSULE_SUPPORTED=y
        (currently commented "is not set"). Rebuild edk2-firmware-tegra.
      - Verify after install: OsIndicationsSupported gains bit 0x04, i.e. reads 0x45.

   b. Capsule build is already wired up (meta-lumen tegra-uefi-capsules_%.bbappend):
      - Produces tegra-bl.cap (bootloader/QSPI) and tegra-kernel.cap.
      - Needed INITRAMFS_IMAGE override (Lumen disables initramfs globally) — keep it.

   c. Decide version-bump policy (see CAPSULE_FW_VERSION in the bbappend):
      - Currently STATIC 0x240501, baked into EVERY build (not skill/flash-time).
      - Static value only proves the FIRST apply; repeated reflashes read the same
        version and UEFI may skip them. For a real update flow, derive
        CAPSULE_FW_VERSION monotonically (git commit count / date) per build, OR
        re-sign with a fresh version at flash time in the flashing skill.
      - Keep --lsv (CAPSULE_LSV) at stock 0x240500 to avoid raising the
        anti-rollback floor.

   d. Chicken-and-egg: installing the FIRST capsule-capable UEFI is itself a QSPI
      write, which the current (capsule-less) UEFI cannot do from Linux. The first
      install MUST go via USB/recovery flashing OR the eth-initrd full-device route
      (QSPI+NVMe). After that one install, future QSPI updates work from Linux via
      capsule-on-disk.

   e. Apply mechanism (capsule-on-disk, the ONLY working Linux path on Orin):
      - Mount active ESP (slot A=/dev/nvme0n1p10, B=/dev/nvme0n1p13; vfat — needs
        fat.ko+vfat.ko, NOT installed on minimal rootfs; add kernel-module-vfat +
        kernel-module-fat to the image, or push the .ko's at flash time).
      - cp tegra-bl.cap to <esp>/EFI/UpdateCapsule/
      - Set OsIndications: write bytes 07 00 00 00 04 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 to
        /sys/firmware/efi/efivars/OsIndications-8be4df61-93ca-11d2-aa0d-00e098032b8c
      - reboot. (Reference: NVIDIA nv_bootloader_capsule_updater.sh.)
      - The /opt/nvidia/l4t-bootloader-config/nv-l4t-bootloader-config.sh on this
        image is a STUB (does not mount the ESP) — mount the ESP yourself.

   f. PROOF OF SUCCESS (verification, all UEFI-reported, content-independent):
      - nvbootctrl dump-slots-info: "Capsule update status" -> 1, A/B slot flips.
      - ESRT /sys/firmware/efi/esrt/entries/entry0/: last_attempt_status==0 (success)
        and last_attempt_version == new CAPSULE_FW_VERSION.

   NOTE: re-enabling capsule support adds ~0 to normal boot time (UEFI just reads an
   EFI var each boot); only the deliberate update boot is longer (one-time QSPI write).

   See also: session checkpoint "QSPI capsule flashing over Ethernet" and
   apply_qspi_capsule.sh (apply tool — apply mechanism must use capsule-on-disk,
   not the runtime /dev/efi_capsule_loader path).

   --- recovery partitions (NVMe) ---
   The eth flasher (flash_eth_initrd.sh, --full-nvme) intentionally SKIPS the
   recovery / recovery-dtb (and _alt) partitions:
     - external-flash.xml assigns them NO <filename> (no source image), and the
       tegraflash package contains no recovery.img — nothing authoritative to write.
     - They are not used by Lumen's boot path (QSPI->UEFI->ESP->extlinux->rootfs);
       only NVIDIA recovery/OTA kernel boot uses them.
   To populate them later: the Yocto build must first emit a real recovery.img
   (recovery kernel + initramfs). Then add one PARTSPEC row in flash_eth_initrd.sh:
       PARTSPEC+=("recovery  $SRC/recovery.img  recovery")
   (plus recovery-dtb if a recovery DTB image is produced).
